    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d467">

                <pb facs="0570" n="1404" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1404"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 656.119/276</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in the Netherlands</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Garrett</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">The Hague</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-03-08T10:00:00+00:19">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 8, 1918, 10 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 12.13 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2112. Minister for Foreign Affairs writes me that there are 65 Dutch ships in the
                    United States. Of these, 39 have now been duly chartered and confirmed by cable;
                    10 duly chartered but not yet confirmed by cable; 3, order to charter cabled but
                    no reply yet; 1 <pb facs="0571" n="1405" xml:id="pg1405"/> offered for charter
                    but no answer received; 2 under German obligation owing to repairs with German
                    material, can therefore be chartered only for Belgian Relief or Switzerland; 5
                    ordered to unload, whereupon to be chartered; 1 passenger ship <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ryndam</hi> and 4 only [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >others?</hi>] not yet agreed upon as to charters. Loudon adds that no cables
                    have been received, for instance, by Netherlands Indian lines which do not even
                    know in which ports their ships are lying. Sheldon notified.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Garrett</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
